Use the system nanobind for the StinkyTofu Python module.

python_module/CMakeLists.txt pulls nanobind with
FetchContent_Declare(nanobind GIT_REPOSITORY ...), which fails outright in a
sandboxed build with no network access.

The module is off by default. rocisa turns it on when gfx1250 is among
GPU_TARGETS, which is also what an empty GPU_TARGETS resolves to, so those
builds stop at "Failed to clone repository". Find the installed nanobind
instead, as the rocisa patch does for rocisa's own bindings; Python is
already located just above.

--- a/python_module/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/python_module/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -43,13 +43,9 @@
 # nanobind requires find_package(Python ...) not Python3
 find_package(Python COMPONENTS Interpreter Development.Module REQUIRED)
 
-include(FetchContent)
-FetchContent_Declare(
-  nanobind
-  GIT_REPOSITORY https://github.com/wjakob/nanobind.git
-  GIT_TAG        9b3afa9dbdc23641daf26fadef7743e7127ff92f # v2.6.1
-)
-FetchContent_MakeAvailable(nanobind)
+# Use the system nanobind instead of fetching it over the network:
+# package-manager builds run sandboxed with no network access.
+find_package(nanobind REQUIRED CONFIG)
 
 # ============================================================================
 # Create the Python Module
